      {
        "id": "f2gxc",
        "attributes": {
          "title": "Eroding Cement of Society? The Effect of Affective Polarization on Trust and Solidarity in a Behavioral Experiment (dataset and documentation)",
          "description": "While trust is the expectation that others will cooperate and forego the temptations of egoism, solidarity is the willingness to help people in need who are victims of outside influences. Trust and solidarity are elemental components of social cohesion and thus constitute the cement of a cooperative society. In this study, we investigate how affective polarization affects trust and solidarity. While most existing research focuses on attitudinal measures in survey research, we conduct an online experiment with a sample of German citizens to examine the behavioral implications of affective polarization in a trust game and a solidarity game. Our participants learn about several characteristics of their counterparts in the experimental games such as their age, gender, state of residence and their party preference. Our findings show that partisanship has a strong effect on trust: Trust is significantly lower among individuals with different party preferences and can be predicted by subjects’ affective polarization. Affective polarization has a significant negative impact on solidarity among individuals, as well. In regard to the German party system, we observe a rift between voters of the right-wing populist AfD and voters of most other legislative parties. While AfD voters display significantly lower levels of trust, voters from other parties are significantly less trustworthy and show less solidarity towards them.",
          "category": "project",
          "custom_citation": null,
          "date_created": "2025-12-11T10:55:48.094926",
